// English text follows the same question order and answer keys as questions.js.
const enText = [{"q":"Who is responsible for the safety of everyone on board?","options":["Only the yacht owner","The skipper in command","The oldest crew member","Whoever is holding the helm"],"why":"The skipper is in command and is responsible for the safety of everyone on board, including themselves."},{"q":"There are six people aboard and five serviceable lifejackets. What follows from the textbook requirement?","options":["Three more are needed because each person needs two","Five suffice if the sixth person can swim","At least one more lifejacket is needed","Five suffice if the skipper stays at the helm"],"why":"Each person needs an available lifejacket; the six-person crew needs at least six."},{"q":"An unconscious person is in the water. Which feature distinguishes a lifejacket from a buoyancy aid?","options":["It helps keep the head and airway above water","It always has an automatic AIS beacon","It replaces a lifebuoy during recovery","It need not be fitted to the wearer"],"why":"A lifejacket is designed to support the head of an unconscious person above water; a buoyancy aid does not reliably do this."},{"q":"You see a red rocket fired from a yacht. How do you treat it?","options":["A course-change warning","An anchorage marker","A distress signal","A harbour-entry acknowledgement"],"why":"A red flare is a distress signal and is particularly visible at night.","alt":"A red signal flare fired above a yacht"},{"q":"You need to mark survivors' position by day for an aircraft crew. Which signal do you use?","options":["A red hand flare","Orange smoke","A white masthead light","An anchor light"],"why":"Orange smoke is a daytime distress signal and also shows wind direction.","alt":"Orange smoke rising from a vessel"},{"q":"Which set creates a \"fire triangle\"?","options":["Fuel, heat, air","Water, electricity, wind","Smoke, flame, sparks","Oil, petrol, gas"],"why":"Most fires require fuel, heat and air to start and sustain."},{"q":"A small area of material is burning in the cabin. Where do you aim the extinguisher?","options":["Above the flames to reduce the air supply","At the ceiling so the agent falls on the fire","At the centre of the visible smoke","At the base of the flames, on the burning material"],"why":"Aim at the base of the fire, where the burning material is, rather than at the smoke or flame tips."},{"q":"Before departure you find that the new crew does not know the safety equipment. What do you do?","options":["Postpone the briefing until the first stop","Check the equipment and show where it is and how to use it","Discuss only the fire extinguishers","Only the skipper needs to know where it is"],"why":"The skipper checks the safety equipment and briefs the crew on its location and use before departure."},{"q":"Which sidelight is on the port side of the yacht?","options":["Green","White","Red","Yellow"],"why":"The port sidelight is red and the starboard sidelight is green.","alt":"Diagram of the red port sidelight sector"},{"q":"A yacht accelerates onto the plane. What distinguishes its hull from a displacement hull?","options":["Speed-generated hydrodynamic lift supports part of its weight","The whole hull stays completely submerged","Propulsion is no longer needed once planing","Hydrostatic buoyancy disappears at every speed"],"why":"At speed, a planing hull gains hydrodynamic lift and rises onto the plane.","alt":"Comparison of displacement and planing hulls on the water"},{"q":"The helmsman of an outboard-powered boat falls overboard. What should a properly attached kill cord do?","options":["Shift into reverse","Start the bilge pump","Disconnect the navigation lights","Stop the engine"],"why":"The kill cord stops the engine when the helmsman moves away from the controls."},{"q":"In which gear should you start the engine?","options":["Forward","Neutral","Reverse","Any gear at low RPM"],"why":"Start the engine with the gear selector in neutral (N)."},{"q":"An outboard has just started. What do you check to confirm its water cooling is working?","options":["A steady stream from the cooling system","Increasing speed on the log","A falling fuel gauge","The stern light switching on"],"why":"After starting, check that cooling water is discharged from the engine."},{"q":"Which drive steers the boat primarily using a separate rudder blade?","options":["An inboard engine with a straight shaft and separate rudder","An outboard motor","A sterndrive","A waterjet"],"why":"With a straight shaft, the propeller provides thrust and a separate rudder steers the boat.","alt":"Diagram of a propeller shaft, propeller, and separate rudder"},{"q":"After trimming the drive up, the bow rises too high. What is the key problem for the helmsman?","options":["Excessive bow immersion","Automatic shift into reverse","Loss of the masthead light","Restricted visibility ahead"],"why":"Excessive trim can raise the bow and restrict the view ahead."},{"q":"Why pause in neutral before shifting from ahead to astern?","options":["To switch on navigation lights","To give the gearbox time to pass through neutral","To lower the anchor","To reset the GPS"],"why":"A brief pause in neutral protects the gearbox when changing direction."},{"q":"You are preparing an outboard with a built-in fuel tank to start. What do you check first?","options":["The vent is closed and throttle fully open","The bilge pump and lights work","Fuel quantity and the tank vent is open","The gear lever is in ahead"],"why":"The starting procedure begins with checking fuel and opening the tank vent."},{"q":"After the first engine start of the day, what do you do with the choke in the textbook procedure?","options":["Turn it off","Leave it on for the entire passage","Use it as a brake","Open the seacock with it"],"why":"The textbook instructs you to turn the choke off after the first successful start."},{"q":"You are ending a trip with an outboard. Under what conditions do you stop it?","options":["On the final approach while still in ahead","After selecting reverse at high revs","After anchoring with the lever in ahead","Once securely moored, in neutral at low revs"],"why":"Secure the boat first, select neutral and low throttle, then stop the engine."},{"q":"What does the abbreviation CEVNI stand for?","options":["A weather forecasting system","The European Code for Inland Waterways","An international flare system","A register of powerboats"],"why":"CEVNI is the European Code for Navigation on Inland Waterways."},{"q":"What determines which side of an inland fairway is marked red or green?","options":["Wind strength","Time of day","The direction of the current and the marking direction","The yacht’s length"],"why":"For the inland route described in the textbook, the marking direction is downstream, towards the sea.","alt":"Green and red buoys marking the fairway boundaries"},{"q":"Looking downstream, what colours mark the inland fairway boundaries?","options":["Green to port, red to starboard","Red to port, green to starboard","White on both sides","Yellow to port, black to starboard"],"why":"Looking downstream, green marks the left boundary and red marks the right boundary.","alt":"Two uncoloured buoys at the fairway boundaries","altOnReveal":"Green and red buoys marking the fairway boundaries"},{"q":"Heading downstream, on which side of your boat should you leave a red starboard-side mark?","options":["To port","Either side","Astern","To starboard"],"why":"Heading downstream, leave the red starboard-side mark to starboard.","alt":"Red buoy marking the starboard boundary of an inland fairway"},{"q":"Heading upstream, on which side of your boat should you leave a red starboard-side mark?","options":["To starboard","To port","Either side if you travel slowly","You may not pass it"],"why":"Heading upstream reverses your viewpoint: leave the red mark to port.","alt":"Red buoy marking the starboard boundary of an inland fairway"},{"q":"The green entry light comes on at a lock. What does it mean?","options":["Entry is permitted","Prepare but wait for another signal","The lock is opening; enter after red appears","Enter only after sounding the horn"],"why":"A green entry signal permits entry to the lock.","alt":"Green light above lock gates"},{"q":"The lock signals that its gates are opening. What do you do?","options":["Enter as soon as the gates are partly open","Follow the first yacht in without waiting for a new signal","Prepare to enter but wait for permission to do so","Enter only in reverse"],"why":"The opening signal means prepare to enter, but wait for permission to enter.","alt":"Red and green lights above lock gates"},{"q":"The illustration shows a spherical mark with horizontal red and green bands. What does it indicate?","options":["The starboard edge of one channel","Safe water all around","An isolated danger","A fairway junction"],"why":"The mark indicates that the fairway divides into two branches.","alt":"Spherical mark with horizontal red and green bands"},{"q":"The illustrated bank marks have horizontally divided colours. What information do they give?","options":["The axis of a crossing to the opposite bank","The channel depth in metres","The direction of stronger current","The fairway's position near that bank and a reference point"],"why":"The textbook says these marks show the fairway relative to the bank and serve as reference points.","alt":"Bank position marks: a diamond divided horizontally and a square with three horizontal bands"},{"q":"The illustration shows a diamond and square divided vertically into black and white. What do they indicate?","options":["A fairway that stays along one bank","Where the fairway crosses to the other bank and the axis of that crossing","Only a ban on mooring at the bank","A point where both banks have equal depth"],"why":"Crossing marks indicate where the fairway changes bank and the axis of the passage.","alt":"Fairway crossing marks: a black and white diamond on the left bank and a black and white square on the right bank"},{"q":"Which region of the IALA system covers Europe?","options":["A","B","C","None"],"why":"The manual assigns Europe to the IALA A region."},{"q":"Entering a port from seaward in IALA Region A, on which side of your boat should you leave a red lateral mark?","options":["To starboard","To port","Either side","Astern without passing it"],"why":"In IALA Region A, a red lateral mark defines the port side of the channel when entering from seaward.","alt":"Red port-hand buoy in IALA Region A"},{"q":"On which side of a west cardinal mark is the safe water?","options":["East","North","West","South"],"why":"A west cardinal mark indicates safe water to its west; pass to the west of the mark.","alt":"Black and yellow west cardinal mark"},{"q":"What does Q(3) mean on a cardinal mark?","options":["A group of three quick flashes","Three long sound signals","A steady light for three minutes","Three colours of light"],"why":"Q means quick flashing; the number in brackets gives the flashes per group.","alt":"Group of three quick light flashes"},{"q":"You are preparing a pilotage plan for an unfamiliar harbour. Which set of details makes it useful in the cockpit?","options":["Courses and distances, with no hazards","Buoy numbers and forecast, with no turning points","Only the harbour name, depth and berth number","Courses, distances, hazards, and buoy colours and numbers"],"why":"A pilotage plan records courses, distances, hazards and buoy colours and numbers for each stage."},{"q":"Two leading marks line up during a harbour approach. What can you infer?","options":["You are on the indicated leading line","Under-keel clearance is sufficient everywhere","No current is setting the boat","Other marks no longer need watching"],"why":"Aligned leading marks show a safe line to follow through the channel.","alt":"Two leading marks aligned on one line"},{"q":"What are sector lights for?","options":["To light the deck","To show tide times","To indicate a channel, turning point, or hazard","Only to identify fishing vessels"],"why":"Coloured light sectors show your position relative to the channel or a hazard.","alt":"Light showing red, white, and green sectors"},{"q":"On a chart, what does the 3 in Fl(3) WRG 15s mean?","options":["Three flashes in each group","Three nautical miles of range","A height of three metres","Three buoys in the channel"],"why":"The number in Fl(3) gives the number of flashes in each group.","alt":"Diagram of the light notation Fl(3) WRG 15s 21m 15M"},{"q":"A safe-course plan sets lighthouse bearing limits of 188°–228°. Which bearing is inside the corridor?","options":["170°","240°","205°","285°"],"why":"205° is between 188° and 228°. The textbook says to monitor the bearing with a hand-bearing compass.","alt":"Safe-course wedge between two limiting bearings"},{"q":"Two motor yachts are crossing; you see the other yacht on your starboard side. Which generally gives way?","options":["The yacht seen to starboard","The faster yacht","Your yacht","Always the yacht nearer shore"],"why":"On crossing courses, the power-driven vessel with the other vessel on its starboard side gives way.","alt":"Two powerboats on crossing courses"},{"q":"Two motor yachts are approaching nearly head-on. What is the standard action?","options":["Both alter course to starboard to pass port-to-port","Both alter course to port to pass starboard-to-starboard","Only the faster yacht turns to port","Both hold course until the last moment"],"why":"Both alter course to starboard so they pass port side to port side.","alt":"Two powerboats approaching head-on"},{"q":"Which vessel gives way when overtaking?","options":["The vessel being overtaken","The overtaking vessel","The slower vessel","The vessel heading into the wind"],"why":"The overtaking vessel keeps clear of the vessel being overtaken.","alt":"A powerboat overtaking another powerboat"},{"q":"How do you treat a sailing yacht that is also using its engine?","options":["As a vessel under sail only","As a vessel with automatic priority","As a ferry","As a power-driven vessel"],"why":"A yacht using mechanical propulsion is treated as a power-driven vessel under the right-of-way rules.","alt":"Black cone with its apex pointing down"},{"q":"A vessel's bearing stays constant while its range decreases. What does this indicate?","options":["A risk of collision","A guaranteed safe passing distance","The vessel is certainly turning to starboard","You can stop observing it"],"why":"A constant bearing with decreasing range indicates collision risk.","alt":"Two approaching vessels and a bearing line"},{"q":"How should you observe your surroundings while sailing?","options":["Only GPS","Only sight","All available means, including sight and hearing","Only radar"],"why":"Keep a proper lookout by sight and hearing and use other available means, such as radar and AIS."},{"q":"Does being the stand-on vessel mean you never need to act to avoid a collision?","options":["Yes, always","Yes, if your yacht is larger","Yes, in good visibility","No; act when the give-way vessel fails to take appropriate action"],"why":"The stand-on vessel must take action if it becomes clear that the give-way vessel is not acting appropriately."},{"q":"According to the inland rules shown in the textbook, which vessel has priority on a river?","options":["The vessel heading upstream","The vessel heading downstream","Always the smaller vessel","Always the vessel closer to shore"],"why":"The textbook’s inland right-of-way diagram gives priority to the downstream vessel.","alt":"Two vessels travelling in opposite directions on a river"},{"q":"A person falls overboard. Which first actions begin a safe recovery?","options":["Stay calm, alert the crew and keep the person in sight","Look away immediately to prepare the radio","Drop the anchor before alerting the crew","Accelerate across the point where the person fell"],"why":"Alarm the crew and keep the person continuously in sight while beginning the recovery."},{"q":"Wind and current act in different directions during a MOB approach. How do you choose the final approach?","options":["Downwind regardless of current","Always from the casualty's lee side","Into the wind or current, whichever has the stronger effect","With the current at high revs to brake quickly"],"why":"Approach into the stronger of the wind or current to keep better control near the person.","alt":"Diagram of a yacht approaching a person overboard"},{"q":"Which part of the drive is especially dangerous to a person in the water?","options":["Tachometer","Propeller","Fuel tank","Compass"],"why":"Keep the person well clear of the propeller during the recovery."},{"q":"You select neutral before a quay, but the yacht keeps moving. Why?","options":["Selecting neutral briefly increases throttle","The rudder stops working only at low revs","Neutral starts the autopilot","The hull retains momentum due to inertia"],"why":"Selecting neutral does not stop the yacht immediately because the boat retains momentum."},{"q":"A yacht alongside a quay moves fore and aft. Which lines limit that motion?","options":["Springs led in opposite directions along the yacht","Only lines led perpendicular to the quay","The anchor rope hanging under the bow","Halyards led from the mast to the quay"],"why":"Bow and stern springs restrain fore-and-aft movement alongside a berth."},{"q":"You berth alongside a pontoon. What minimum number of fenders per side does the textbook recommend?","options":["One amidships","Two, only at bow and stern","At least three per side","One per mooring line regardless of boat length"],"why":"The textbook recommends at least three fenders when berthing alongside."},{"q":"The boat drifts toward a pontoon and a fender has shifted. What must you never use to push the hull away?","options":["A fender placed between hull and pontoon","Hands or feet","A boat hook used from a safe position","The engine during a cautious manoeuvre"],"why":"Hands and feet can be crushed between the boat and the quay."},{"q":"How should you approach a berth in a marina?","options":["As fast as possible before the wind affects you","Without fenders if the berth is visible","Always with the wind astern","Slowly, after checking the berth, with fenders ready on both sides"],"why":"Inspect the berth, approach slowly, and have fenders ready."},{"q":"After a MOB alarm one crew member continuously points at the person in the water. What is their main purpose?","options":["Operating the radio throughout the manoeuvre","Keeping the casualty in sight and showing the helmsman their position","Preparing the anchor on the bow","Checking the crew list below deck"],"why":"Assign a lookout to keep the casualty in sight and point out their position to the helmsman."},{"q":"A person has just fallen overboard. What do you throw to provide buoyancy and mark the position?","options":["A light throwing line without a float","A fender still tied to the side","A lifebuoy or horseshoe buoy","A lifejacket still inside its cover"],"why":"Throw a lifebuoy or horseshoe buoy; it gives buoyancy and helps mark the casualty's position."},{"q":"A headcount reveals a missing crew member who is not visible in the water. What do you do?","options":["Alert the crew, search the yacht, slow down, retrace the route and send MAYDAY","Keep course to the next harbour to report it there","Search alone for a long time before alerting the crew","Send PAN PAN but do not change course"],"why":"The textbook directs the crew to search the yacht, slow down, reverse course and send a MAYDAY."},{"q":"Which weather details matter before departure?","options":["Only the cabin temperature","Wind speed and direction, precipitation, pressure, and signs of change","Only sunset time","Only last week’s forecast"],"why":"Get current local information about wind, precipitation, pressure, and changing conditions."},{"q":"The forecast gives force 4 Beaufort. What wind speed range does the textbook table give?","options":["7–10 kn","17–21 kn","11–16 kn","22–27 kn"],"why":"The textbook's Beaufort table gives 11–16 knots for force 4."},{"q":"You approach an anchoring spot in wind. How do you usually approach?","options":["Slowly into the wind so you can lose way","Downwind to maximise speed","Beam-on to the wind regardless of current","Any way if the chain is long enough"],"why":"Approach into the wind and stop before lowering the anchor.","alt":"Diagram of a yacht approaching an anchorage into the wind"},{"q":"You anchor in 4 m of water using chain. How much chain does the textbook recommend?","options":["4–8 m","8–12 m","28–40 m","12–20 m"],"why":"For chain, use three to five times the water depth: 12–20 m at 4 m depth."},{"q":"You anchor in 4 m of water using rope. How much rope does the textbook recommend?","options":["16–24 m","28–40 m","12–20 m","40–60 m"],"why":"For rope, use seven to ten times the water depth: 28–40 m at 4 m depth."},{"q":"You plan a passage to an unfamiliar harbour. Which plan covers both the route and contingencies?","options":["Only destination and fuel reserve","Only the GPS course","Route, weather, hazards, crew roles and a contingency plan","Only wind forecast and departure time"],"why":"Plan the route and conditions, assign crew roles, and prepare a contingency option."},{"q":"What should you do with the passage plan underway?","options":["Monitor progress and update the plan regularly","Put it away after leaving port","Keep it from the crew","Change course only from memory"],"why":"A passage plan is a living document; check your position and progress regularly."},{"q":"Why use leading marks when entering or leaving a port?","options":["To check the colour of the fuel","To count passengers","To measure wind speed","To check that you remain on track despite tidal current"],"why":"Tidal current may set the boat off course; leading marks help reveal when you leave the approach line.","alt":"Two leading marks aligned on one line"},{"q":"Why does cold air move beneath warm air at a front?","options":["Because it is more humid","Because it is denser","Because it always comes from the north","Because it cannot cool"],"why":"Cold air is denser, so it pushes beneath warm air.","alt":"Diagram of two weather fronts"},{"q":"Which front usually moves faster in the textbook diagram?","options":["Warm front","Neither moves","Both always move at the same speed","Cold front"],"why":"The textbook states that a cold front moves faster than a warm front.","alt":"Diagram of two weather fronts"},{"q":"Why might inland water not look exactly like the Beaufort description in the table?","options":["The scale mainly describes observed conditions at sea","Beaufort force applies only at night","There is no wind inland","The scale measures temperature only"],"why":"The textbook notes that the Beaufort scale describes sea conditions, which may look different on inland waters."},{"q":"A depth sounder reads 2.0 m on an unfamiliar yacht. What must you know to assess under-keel clearance?","options":["Whether the screen is backlit","Whether it counts engine hours","Whether its sensor connects to the log","Whether it is referenced to the water surface or hull bottom"],"why":"Know the sounder's calibration reference before using its depth reading to judge clearance."},{"q":"An electronic compass works with the autopilot. How do you treat it relative to a magnetic compass?","options":["It replaces the magnetic compass after autopilot calibration","It is accurate only with GPS turned off","It supplements rather than replaces the magnetic compass","It displays only true heading"],"why":"An electronic compass supplements, but does not replace, a magnetic compass."},{"q":"You navigate with a small-screen chartplotter. What risk comes from an insufficiently detailed chart scale?","options":["Some hazards may not be visible","Zooming makes GPS stop reporting position","The display changes true heading to magnetic","All buoy colours change on the screen"],"why":"At a given scale, a small chartplotter display may hide hazards that are visible on a larger chart."},{"q":"What should you check before launching a boat from a trailer?","options":["Drain plugs fitted, with a mooring line and fenders ready","That the trailer lights are underwater","That the winch strap was removed in the car park","That every passenger is standing behind the trailer"],"why":"Before launching, check drain plugs and prepare a line and fenders. Keep the winch strap attached until launch."},{"q":"Where should you stand while winching a boat onto its trailer?","options":["In line with the taut winch strap","Clear of the line of the winch strap","Directly in front of the bow","Under the boat"],"why":"A taut strap can snap; keep clear of its line."},{"q":"What should you do with toilet waste on inland waterways?","options":["Discharge it outside the fairway","Discharge it only at high speed","Do not discharge it into the water; use a pump-out facility","Dilute it with seawater"],"why":"The textbook prohibits discharging toilet waste into inland waters."},{"q":"What should be included in a crew safety briefing?","options":["Only the meal plan","Only the route to the marina","Only the berth allocation","Location of lifesaving equipment, fire and MOB procedures, and use of the radio"],"why":"The safety briefing covers equipment, emergency procedures, the engine, seacocks, and VHF."},{"q":"What should you check before starting an inboard engine?","options":["Oil level, fuel, hoses, and the cooling-water seacock","The colour of the harbour flag","The berth number on the chart","Only the time of high tide"],"why":"Before starting, check items including oil, fuel, hoses, battery, and the cooling-water seacock."},{"q":"What does the electronic log’s Speed/Log function measure in the textbook?","options":["Wind speed","Boat speed through the water","Wave height","Fuel level"],"why":"The Speed/Log description gives the vessel’s speed through the water."},{"q":"How much food and drink does the checklist recommend for the planned passage?","options":["Enough for the full passage plus 20% extra","Enough for half the passage","Only for time in port","Only for the skipper"],"why":"The checklist recommends supplies for 100% of the passage plus a 20% margin."},{"q":"IALA: what does this cardinal mark indicate?","options":["Safe water lies north of the mark","Safe water lies south of the mark","Isolated danger","Right side of the channel"],"why":"The topmarks and black over yellow bands identify a north cardinal mark. Pass to the north of it."},{"q":"IALA: what does this cardinal mark indicate?","options":["Safe water lies west of the mark","Safe water lies east of the mark","Left side of the channel","Safe water all around"],"why":"The topmarks and black-yellow-black bands identify a east cardinal mark. Pass to the east of it."},{"q":"IALA: what does this cardinal mark indicate?","options":["Safe water lies north of the mark","Marks the anchorage","Safe water lies south of the mark","Indicates a special mark"],"why":"The topmarks and yellow over black bands identify a south cardinal mark. Pass to the south of it."},{"q":"IALA: what does this cardinal mark indicate?","options":["Eastern side of the hazard","Isolated Danger","End of fairway","Safe water on west side of mark"],"why":"The topmarks and yellow-black-yellow bands identify a west cardinal mark. Pass to the west of it."},{"q":"IALA Region A: what does this red buoy mark?","options":["The port side of the channel when entering from seaward","The starboard side when entering from seaward","Safe water on all sides","An isolated danger"],"why":"In IALA Region A, a red can buoy marks the port side of a channel when entering from seaward."},{"q":"IALA Region A: what does this green buoy mark?","options":["The port side of the channel when entering from seaward","The starboard side when entering from seaward","A north cardinal mark","A new wreck"],"why":"In IALA Region A, a green conical buoy marks the starboard side of a channel when entering from seaward."},{"q":"IALA: what does this buoy mean?","options":["Safe water on all sides","An area with special regulations","An isolated danger","The starboard side of the channel"],"why":"Black and red bands with two black ball topmarks indicate an isolated danger."},{"q":"IALA: what does this buoy mean?","options":["No wash","The port side of the channel","A south cardinal mark","Safe water all around the mark"],"why":"Vertical red and white stripes with one red ball topmark indicate safe water all around."},{"q":"IALA: what does a yellow buoy with an X topmark indicate?","options":["A special mark for an area or feature with a specific purpose","An isolated danger","The starboard side of the channel","An east cardinal mark"],"why":"Yellow with an X topmark identifies a special mark. Check the chart for its specific purpose."},{"q":"CEVNI: what does this sign mean?","options":["Overtaking is mandatory","No overtaking","Proceed to starboard only","Maximum speed 12 km/h"],"why":"The crossed-out vessel symbols mean overtaking is prohibited."},{"q":"CEVNI: what does this sign mean?","options":["Sound signal required","No anchoring","No wash","Swimming area"],"why":"Crossed-out waves mean no wash. Reduce speed enough to avoid creating a damaging wake."},{"q":"CEVNI: what does this sign mean?","options":["Mooring required","VHF prohibited","Boat launching area","No mooring to the bank on the side where the sign stands"],"why":"The crossed-out mooring symbol prohibits mooring to the bank on the side where the sign stands."},{"q":"CEVNI: what does this sign require?","options":["Do not exceed 12 km/h","Maintain at least 12 knots","Keep 12 metres clear","Proceed for 12 minutes"],"why":"The number on this mandatory sign sets a maximum speed of 12 km/h."},{"q":"CEVNI: what does this sign require?","options":["Switch off VHF","Keep a VHF radio watch","Change to channel 12","Sound the horn"],"why":"VHF on a mandatory sign requires a listening watch on VHF radio."},{"q":"CEVNI: what does this sign require?","options":["Stop the vessel","Stop the engine","Sound a signal","Proceed at 12 km/h"],"why":"A black dot on this mandatory sign requires a sound signal."},{"q":"CEVNI: heading downstream, which channel boundary does this green buoy mark?","options":["The right boundary","The left boundary","The centre of the channel","A bifurcation"],"why":"Looking downstream, a green conical mark identifies the left boundary of the inland channel."},{"q":"CEVNI: heading downstream, which channel boundary does this red buoy mark?","options":["The centre of the channel","A bifurcation","The right boundary","The left boundary"],"why":"Looking downstream, a red cylindrical mark identifies the right boundary of the inland channel."},{"q":"CEVNI: heading upstream past this green buoy, on which side of your boat should you leave it?","options":["To port","Astern","Ahead","To starboard"],"why":"Heading upstream reverses your viewpoint. Leave the green mark to starboard."},{"q":"CEVNI: heading upstream past this red buoy, on which side of your boat should you leave it?","options":["To port","To starboard","Ahead","It depends only on the wind"],"why":"Heading upstream reverses your viewpoint. Leave the red mark to port."},{"q":"CEVNI: heading downstream, where is the channel between these green and red buoys?","options":["Left of the green buoy","Between the buoys","Right of the red buoy","Outside both marks"],"why":"Pass between the lateral marks: heading downstream, green stays to port and red to starboard."},{"q":"CEVNI: what does a buoy with horizontal red and green bands mean?","options":["Safe water mark","No passage","A channel bifurcation","Right boundary of the channel"],"why":"Horizontal red and green bands identify a channel bifurcation."},{"q":"CEVNI: heading upstream towards a bifurcation, what does the red cylindrical topmark show?","options":["Both branches are closed","The main channel leads right","You may only travel downstream","The main channel leads left"],"why":"In the upstream direction shown in the textbook, a red cylindrical topmark indicates that the main channel leads left."},{"q":"CEVNI: heading upstream towards a bifurcation, what does the green conical topmark show?","options":["The main channel leads right","The main channel leads left","The bifurcation is closed","An isolated danger lies beyond"],"why":"In the upstream direction shown in the textbook, a green conical topmark indicates that the main channel leads right."},{"q":"CEVNI: what does one diamond above a recommended bridge passage mean?","options":["No passage","Recommended passage in both directions","Passage only downstream","No overtaking"],"why":"One diamond marks a recommended bridge passage usable in both directions."},{"q":"CEVNI: what do two diamonds above a bridge passage mean?","options":["Recommended passage only in the indicated direction","Recommended passage in both directions","Stop required","Isolated danger"],"why":"Two diamonds mark a recommended passage only in the indicated direction; passage in the opposite direction is prohibited."},{"q":"CEVNI: what does this sign with a horizontal bar require?","options":["Sound a signal","Proceed with particular caution","Stop the vessel","Proceed to starboard"],"why":"A horizontal bar on this mandatory sign means the vessel must stop."},{"q":"CEVNI: what does this sign with a vertical bar require?","options":["Stop the vessel","Send SOS","Proceed with particular caution","Alter course to port"],"why":"A vertical bar on this mandatory sign requires particular caution."},{"q":"What arc does the red port sidelight cover?","options":["90°","112.5°","135°","225°"],"why":"The red port sidelight covers 112.5°: from dead ahead to 22.5° abaft the port beam."},{"q":"What arc does the green starboard sidelight cover?","options":["135°","225°","112.5°","360°"],"why":"The green starboard sidelight covers 112.5°: from dead ahead to 22.5° abaft the starboard beam."},{"q":"What arc does the white masthead light cover?","options":["112.5°","135°","180°","225°"],"why":"The masthead light covers 225° ahead: 112.5° to each side of the bow."},{"q":"What arc does the white stern light cover?","options":["135°","112.5°","225°","360°"],"why":"The stern light covers a 135° arc astern."},{"q":"What total arc do both sidelights cover together?","options":["135°","225°","112.5°","360°"],"why":"Each sidelight covers 112.5°, so together they cover 225° around the bow."},{"q":"What full arc do the masthead and stern lights cover together?","options":["225°","135°","360°","270°"],"why":"The 225° masthead arc and 135° stern arc add up to 360° around the boat."},{"q":"In the example Q(3)GR3M, what does Q(3) mean?","options":["Three prolonged sound signals","Three buoy colours","A range of three miles","A group of three quick flashes"],"why":"Q means quick flashing and (3) means a group of three flashes. This fragment alone does not identify the mark’s position."},{"q":"In the example Q(3)GR3M, what do G and R denote?","options":["Types of sound signal","Green and red light colours","Two current directions","Two speed units"],"why":"G is green and R is red. The letters alone do not show the sector layout or prove alternating colours; check the full chart description."},{"q":"In the example Q(3)GR3M, what does the final 3M mean?","options":["Light height of 3 metres","A period of 3 minutes","A nominal range of 3 nautical miles","Three sound signals"],"why":"In light descriptions, capital M denotes nautical miles of nominal range; lowercase m denotes metres of height."},{"q":"In Fl(3)WRG.15s21m15M, what does Fl(3) mean?","options":["Three flashes in a group","Three sound signals","A range of three miles","A fixed light"],"why":"Fl means a flashing light; (3) gives the number of flashes in each group."},{"q":"In Fl(3)WRG.15s21m15M, what does WRG mean?","options":["Wind, river, and depth","White, red, and green light sectors","Three sides of a buoy","No passage"],"why":"W, R and G stand for white, red and green. In this example they describe the sector-light colours."},{"q":"In Fl(3)WRG.15s21m15M, what does 15s mean?","options":["A height of 15 metres","A range of 15 miles","15 sound signals","The light pattern repeats every 15 seconds"],"why":"15s is the period of the full light sequence: the pattern repeats every 15 seconds."},{"q":"In Fl(3)WRG.15s21m15M, what does 21m mean?","options":["Light height of 21 metres","A range of 21 nautical miles","21 flashes in a group","A speed of 21 knots"],"why":"Lowercase m here gives the light height in metres."},{"q":"In Fl(3)WRG.15s21m15M, what does the final 15M mean?","options":["Light height of 15 metres","A nominal range of 15 nautical miles","A period of 15 minutes","15 flashes in a group"],"why":"Capital M denotes nautical miles of nominal range."},{"q":"IALA: which cardinal mark shows groups of three quick white flashes, Q(3)?","options":["North","South","East","West"],"why":"The east cardinal mark has a group of three quick white flashes. Also check its colours and topmarks."},{"q":"IALA: which cardinal mark shows six quick flashes followed by one long flash?","options":["East","North","West","South"],"why":"The south cardinal mark shows Q(6) + LFl: six quick flashes followed by one long flash."},{"q":"IALA: which cardinal mark shows a group of nine quick flashes, Q(9)?","options":["West","East","North","South"],"why":"The west cardinal mark has a group of nine quick white flashes."},{"q":"IALA: which cardinal mark has continuous quick flashes rather than groups of 3, 6, or 9?","options":["South","North","East","West"],"why":"The north cardinal mark has continuous quick or very quick flashes."},{"q":"What does the Iso rhythm in this diagram mean?","options":["Light and darkness have equal durations","Three quick flashes","A continuously lit light","One prolonged sound signal"],"why":"Iso is an isophase light: the light and dark intervals have equal duration."},{"q":"CEVNI: what does the single short blast shown mean?","options":["I am altering course to starboard","I am altering course to port","I am operating astern propulsion","I cannot manoeuvre"],"why":"One short blast on inland waters indicates an alteration of course to starboard."},{"q":"CEVNI: what do two short blasts mean?","options":["I am altering course to starboard","I am altering course to port","I need help","I am stopping in a lock"],"why":"Two short blasts indicate an alteration of course to port."},{"q":"CEVNI: what do three short blasts mean?","options":["I am altering course to port","I am overtaking","I am operating astern propulsion","I am sending SOS"],"why":"Three short blasts indicate that the vessel is operating astern propulsion."},{"q":"CEVNI: what do four short blasts mean in the textbook diagram?","options":["I am entering port","I am heading downstream","I will alter course to starboard","I cannot manoeuvre"],"why":"In the textbook’s CEVNI diagram, four short blasts mean “I cannot manoeuvre.”"},{"q":"COLREG: what do five or more short, rapid blasts mean?","options":["I am in doubt about the other vessel’s intentions or collision-avoidance action","I am altering course to port","I am reporting my draught","I mark safe water"],"why":"Under COLREG Rule 34(d), at least five short, rapid blasts signal doubt about another vessel’s intentions or action to avoid collision. This is a maritime signal, distinct from the four-blast CEVNI example."},{"q":"Which distress signal is three short, three long, then three short?","options":["Q(3)","An alteration-of-course signal","SOS in Morse code","A lock-entry signal"],"why":"SOS is ··· ––– ··· in Morse code and is used to call for help in distress."},{"q":"How long is a short blast in the textbook’s CEVNI diagram?","options":["About 1 second","About 4 seconds","About 15 seconds","One minute"],"why":"The textbook shows a short blast lasting about one second."},{"q":"How long is a prolonged blast in the textbook’s CEVNI diagram?","options":["About 1 second","About 4 seconds","About 15 seconds","One minute"],"why":"The textbook shows a prolonged blast lasting about four seconds."},{"q":"What fog signal does a power-driven vessel making way through the water sound?","options":["One prolonged blast at intervals of no more than 2 minutes","Two short blasts every minute","Three bell strokes","One continuous blast for a minute"],"why":"The textbook shows one prolonged blast at intervals of no more than 2 minutes.","alt":"Diagram of one prolonged sound signal"},{"q":"A power-driven vessel is under way but stopped and making no way through the water. What fog signal does it sound?","options":["One short blast","Two prolonged blasts at intervals of no more than 2 minutes","Nine rapid blasts","Three bell strokes"],"why":"Two prolonged blasts identify a vessel under way that is not making way through the water.","alt":"Diagram of two prolonged sound signals"},{"q":"What is the basic fog signal of a vessel at anchor?","options":["One prolonged blast every 2 minutes","Two short blasts","Rapid bell ringing at intervals of no more than 1 minute","Four short blasts"],"why":"The textbook specifies rapid bell ringing at intervals of no more than 1 minute; vessels over 100 m also sound a gong.","alt":"Bell signal for a vessel at anchor in fog"},{"q":"How does the bell fog signal of a vessel aground differ from that of a vessel at anchor?","options":["One short whistle blast","No bell","Nine rapid strokes","Three distinct bell strokes before and after rapid ringing"],"why":"A vessel aground adds three distinct bell strokes before and after the rapid ringing.","alt":"Bell with extra strokes before and after the signal"},{"q":"What day shape does a sailing yacht show when also using its engine?","options":["A black ball","A black cone with its apex down","Two black diamonds","Flag A"],"why":"The textbook shows a black cone, apex down, for a sailing vessel also using its engine.","alt":"Black cone with its apex pointing down"},{"q":"What do two black balls in a vertical line mean as a day shape?","options":["Vessel at anchor","Vessel aground","Vessel not under command","Vessel with a diver below"],"why":"Two black balls in a vertical line identify a vessel not under command.","alt":"Two black balls in a vertical line"},{"q":"You see flag A on a vessel. What does it indicate?","options":["I have a diver down; keep well clear and proceed slowly","I carry dangerous goods","I am entering a lock","I am altering course to port"],"why":"In the textbook diagram, flag A indicates diving activity and calls for clearance and slow speed.","alt":"White and blue signal flag A"},{"q":"What does red flag B mean in the textbook flag-code diagram?","options":["Vessel at anchor","Recommended bridge passage","End of voyage","I am loading, discharging or carrying dangerous goods"],"why":"The textbook links flag B with loading, discharging or carrying dangerous goods.","alt":"Red signal flag B"},{"q":"Which day shape identifies a vessel restricted in her ability to manoeuvre?","options":["One black ball","Two black cones","Ball, diamond and ball in a vertical line","Three black diamonds"],"why":"Ball–diamond–ball is the day shape of a vessel restricted in her ability to manoeuvre.","alt":"Black ball, diamond, and ball in a vertical line"},{"q":"What is the ISSA logbook mentioned in the textbook?","options":["An electronic speed log","A chart of sector lights","A sailing record book used to document training and experience","A list of CEVNI signs"],"why":"The textbook describes the ISSA logbook as a sailing record book in a format designed to be filled in."},{"q":"A person falls over the port side of a small boat. Which way do you make the first decisive turn?","options":["To starboard to move the stern away","To port, towards the person","Straight ahead until the person disappears from view","Only after the propeller has fully stopped"],"why":"The initial turn towards the person moves the stern and propeller away before the return manoeuvre."},{"q":"In the textbook MOB manoeuvre for a small boat, after what fraction of a circle do you slow down?","options":["About 1/4 of a circle","About 1/2 of a circle","About 2/3 of a circle","Only after a full circle"],"why":"The small-boat procedure says to slow down after about two-thirds of a circle."},{"q":"When do you select neutral again before the final MOB approach?","options":["When the person is about 15° off the bow","When the person is directly astern","Only once the boat is alongside them","Immediately after the first turn"],"why":"The textbook calls for neutral when the person is about 15 degrees off the bow."},{"q":"After the first outboard start of the day, what sequence applies to the choke and kill cord?","options":["Leave the choke on and the cord unattached","Turn off the choke, attach the cord to yourself and test it","Select reverse before turning off the choke","Attach the cord only to the throttle"],"why":"After the first start, turn off the choke, attach the kill cord and test the emergency stop."},{"q":"On a straight-shaft boat in reverse, the propeller rotates clockwise. Which way does the stern move before the yacht moves astern?","options":["To port","To starboard","It does not move","Always into the wind"],"why":"The textbook says clockwise propeller rotation in reverse moves the stern to starboard."},{"q":"For which arrangement does the textbook describe prop walk during manoeuvres?","options":["A straight shaft while operating in reverse","Any drive, only in ahead","Only an outboard in neutral","A waterjet while stationary"],"why":"The effect is described for a straight-shaft boat using reverse."},{"q":"You anchor in 5 m of water with rope. What range follows from the 7–10 times depth recommendation?","options":["15–25 m","25–35 m","35–50 m","50–75 m"],"why":"Seven to ten times 5 m gives 35–50 m of rope."},{"q":"How does the textbook say to check that the anchor has set after paying out rope or chain?","options":["Run ahead at high revs for a minute","Use reverse at higher revs for 2–3 seconds","Stop the engine and go below immediately","Raise the anchor and inspect its colour"],"why":"After paying out the proper length, use reverse at higher revs for two to three seconds."},{"q":"When choosing an anchoring spot, what must you consider besides depth?","options":["Only distance from the marina","The yacht’s swinging room and possible wind shifts","Only the direction you arrived from","Only the seabed colour shown on the chart"],"why":"The yacht can swing at anchor as wind changes; that area must be clear."},{"q":"A depth sounder is calibrated to the hull bottom rather than the water surface. What does its reading represent?","options":["Depth from water surface to seabed","Distance from hull bottom to seabed","The yacht’s draught","Wave height under the hull"],"why":"With this offset the reading is referenced to the hull bottom; know the setting when judging clearance."},{"q":"What should you do regularly with an electronic speed log sensor?","options":["Calibrate it only while moored","Check and clean it because it can clog","Disconnect it before every marina entry","Replace it after every engine start"],"why":"The small sensor can clog and should be checked and cleaned regularly."},{"q":"An electronic compass differs from the magnetic compass reading. Which setting should you check?","options":["Whether it displays true rather than magnetic heading","Whether it displays speed over ground","Whether it connects to the depth sounder","Whether the VHF antenna is disconnected"],"why":"The electronic compass can be set to show true or magnetic heading."},{"q":"What is a fog signal in this quiz?","options":["A sidelight flash in fog","A sound made in restricted visibility","A sector light colour","A black day shape on the mast"],"why":"A fog signal is a sound, such as a horn or bell; a light has its own characteristic."},{"q":"The illustration shows one prolonged horn blast. Which vessel may make it in fog?","options":["A power-driven vessel making way","A power-driven vessel underway but stopped in the water","A vessel at anchor","A vessel aground"],"why":"One prolonged blast at intervals not exceeding two minutes is the signal for a power-driven vessel making way.","alt":"Fog-signal pattern to identify"},{"q":"The illustration shows two prolonged horn blasts. What do they mean in fog?","options":["A power-driven vessel is making way","A power-driven vessel is underway but not making way","A vessel is anchored","A vessel is aground"],"why":"Two prolonged blasts indicate a power-driven vessel underway but not making way.","alt":"Fog-signal pattern to identify"},{"q":"A power-driven vessel is making way in restricted visibility. What is the maximum interval between its basic signals?","options":["15 seconds","1 minute","2 minutes","5 minutes"],"why":"The textbook gives one prolonged blast at intervals not exceeding two minutes."},{"q":"A vessel over 100 m long is anchored in fog. What follows the rapid ringing of its bell?","options":["One short horn blast","Rapid sounding of a gong","Six quick flashes","Two prolonged horn blasts"],"why":"The textbook adds rapid gong sounding after the bell for an anchored vessel over 100 m."},{"q":"You hear three distinct bell strokes, rapid ringing, then three more strokes. What vessel state does this indicate?","options":["At anchor","Aground","Power-driven and making way","Power-driven and stopped in the water"],"why":"Three separate strokes before and after rapid ringing distinguish an aground vessel from an anchored one.","alt":"Fog-signal pattern to identify"},{"q":"What is the maximum interval for the basic bell signal of an anchored or aground vessel?","options":["15 seconds","30 seconds","1 minute","2 minutes"],"why":"The textbook gives intervals not exceeding one minute for these bell signals."},{"q":"What fog signal does the textbook show for a vessel not under command?","options":["One prolonged followed by two short blasts","Two prolonged blasts","Three short blasts","Rapid ringing of a bell"],"why":"A vessel not under command sounds one prolonged followed by two short blasts at intervals not exceeding two minutes.","alt":"Fog-signal pattern to identify"},{"q":"What fog signal does the textbook show for a vessel constrained by her draught?","options":["One prolonged followed by two short blasts","One prolonged blast only","Two prolonged blasts","Three separate bell strokes"],"why":"The textbook shows one prolonged followed by two short blasts for a vessel constrained by her draught."},{"q":"You hear one prolonged and two short blasts. What can you tell from that rhythm alone in the table?","options":["It must be a vessel not under command","It must be a vessel constrained by her draught","More than one vessel category uses this rhythm","It is an anchored vessel"],"why":"The table shows the same rhythm for more than one category, including not under command and constrained by draught."},{"q":"Which optional Morse signals does the textbook mention for vessels at anchor and aground?","options":["Anchor: A; aground: R","Anchor: R; aground: A","Anchor: SOS; aground: Q","Only SOS in both cases"],"why":"The textbook mentions optional Morse A for an anchored vessel and Morse R for an aground vessel."},{"q":"A chart shows Horn(1)15s beside a navigation mark. What does it describe?","options":["One horn blast from the mark every 15 seconds","One light flash every 15 seconds","A sound every passing yacht must make every 15 seconds","Fifteen bell strokes from an anchored vessel"],"why":"Horn(1)15s describes the navigation mark’s horn, not a signal required from every passing yacht."}];
